 the following year Headlam became commander of the RAF Training Command after the incumbent, Group Captain John "Tubby" Crossley, returned to England a little earlier than expected due to the illness of his wife.^{[1]}^{[2]}^{[3]}

He was promoted to acting air vice-marshal on 15 October 1955 and, three days later, was assigned to command the RAF Training Command on a substantive basis, taking over from Crossley.^{[4]}^{[5]} In January 1956 Headlam attended a meeting of all commanders of RAF stations in Cyprus which included the two commanders of the Cyprus Base Defence Force, along with the military secretary to the Governor of Cyprus, and representatives of the British Army of the Rhine (BAR), the Cyprus Police, the Central Intelligence Agency, and the Joint Services Operations Room; the object of the meeting was to ensure that "the necessary precautions were being taken to protect the base from external attack from any quarter."^{[6]}

